---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import hp from '../../data/heat-pump.json';

const OCTOPUS_REFERRAL = 'https://share.octopus.energy/lush-spark-49';

const { hardware, cost, demand, annual_gbp: a, payback_years: p, sensitivity: s, gas_price: g } = hp;
const gbp = (n: number) => `£${Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-GB')}`;
const netCapital = cost.paid_gbp - cost.avoided_boiler_gbp;
const gasBillMarket = a.with_gas_gas_market + a.with_gas_standing;
const gasBillFixed = a.with_gas_gas_fixed + a.with_gas_standing;

// shade the sensitivity grid: pale where the sum barely works, ink where it clearly does
const allSavings = s.rows.flatMap((r: any) => r.saving);
const lo = Math.min(...allSavings);
const hi = Math.max(...allSavings);
const shade = (v: number) => {
  const k = (v - lo) / (hi - lo);
  if (k > 0.72) return '#5b83e6';
  if (k > 0.45) return '#9ab4f2';
  if (k > 0.22) return '#c9d8f8';
  return '#e8eefc';
};
const ink = (v: number) => ((v - lo) / (hi - lo) > 0.72 ? '#fff' : 'inherit');
const marketRow = s.rows.find((r: any) => r.gas_p === g.market_p);
---

<Base
  title="The heat pump"
  description="A 19-year-old gas boiler replaced by an Octopus Cosy 9 air source heat pump in December 2026. What it saves once the extra electricity is counted, using the market gas price instead of our fix, which ends soon."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>The heat pump</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The gas boiler is 19 years old, heat-only, and sits in a garage we're converting into an
      office, so it has had a good innings. In <strong>December 2026</strong> an
      <strong>Octopus Cosy 9</strong> replaces it, the gas meter comes out, and the house goes
      fully electric. These are the sums we did beforehand, including the extra electricity the
      heat pump uses, which a lot of savings estimates leave out.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p><strong>Everything on this page is modelled, not measured.</strong> The install hasn't
    happened yet. Heat demand comes from our own historic gas meter readings, and the efficiency
    figure is the manufacturer's at the quoted flow temperature. Unlike the
    <a href="/stats/">stats</a>, none of it has been measured. After the first winter we'll replace
    it with real numbers, and you'll be able to see how far out we were.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tiles" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">What we pay</p>
      <span class="figure md">{gbp(cost.paid_gbp)}</span>
      <p class="note-line">after the {gbp(cost.bus_grant_gbp)} grant</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Heat needed</p>
      <span class="figure md">{(demand.heat_kwh / 1000).toFixed(0)}<span class="unit">MWh/yr</span></span>
      <p class="note-line">space + hot water</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Gas bill removed</p>
      <span class="figure md">{gbp(gasBillMarket)}</span>
      <p class="note-line">at today's {g.market_p}p gas</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Actual saving</p>
      <span class="figure md accent">{gbp(a.saving_at_market_gas)}<span class="unit">/yr</span></span>
      <p class="note-line">after the new electricity</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>Which gas price?</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    The gas price makes more difference than anything else, so we'll start there. We're fixed at
    {' '}<strong>{g.fixed_p}p/kWh</strong> until <strong>March 2027</strong>, and the market average
    is already <strong>{g.market_p}p</strong>. A cheap fix that ends in a few months would make the
    boiler look better than it is, so the main figures on this page all use the market price. The
    fixed-price figures are shown alongside for reference.
  </p>

  <div class="cols-2">
    <div class="card">
      <p class="kicker">At our fixed {g.fixed_p}p · until Mar 2027</p>
      <span class="figure md">{gbp(a.saving_at_fixed_gas)}<span class="unit">/yr</span></span>
      <p class="note-line" style="margin-top:0.5rem">
        Gas bill {gbp(gasBillFixed)}. Payback {p.at_fixed.raw} years on the raw outlay.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="kicker">At the market {g.market_p}p · what we'll actually face</p>
      <span class="figure md accent">{gbp(a.saving_at_market_gas)}<span class="unit">/yr</span></span>
      <p class="note-line" style="margin-top:0.5rem">
        Gas bill {gbp(gasBillMarket)}. Payback {p.at_market.raw} years on the raw outlay.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>The extra electricity</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    Getting rid of a {gbp(gasBillMarket)} gas bill doesn't save {gbp(gasBillMarket)}. The heat pump
    makes the same heat from electricity, which is
    {' '}<strong>{demand.hp_electricity_kwh.toLocaleString('en-GB')} kWh</strong> of new demand,
    most of it in the four coldest months. Once that's paid for, well over half the gas saving has
    gone.
  </p>

  <div class="cols-2">
    <div class="card">
      <p class="kicker">Keep the gas boiler</p>
      <div class="ledger" style="margin-top:0.6rem">
        <div class="row"><span class="k">Electricity</span><span class="v">{gbp(a.with_gas_electricity)}</span></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="k">Gas · {demand.gas_now_kwh.toLocaleString('en-GB')} kWh at {g.market_p}p</span><span class="v">{gbp(a.with_gas_gas_market)}</span></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="k">Gas standing charge</span><span class="v">{gbp(a.with_gas_standing)}</span></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="k"><strong>Total</strong></span><span class="v"><strong>{gbp(a.with_gas_total_market)}</strong></span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="kicker">Heat pump, no gas at all</p>
      <div class="ledger" style="margin-top:0.6rem">
        <div class="row"><span class="k">Electricity</span><span class="v">{gbp(a.with_hp_total)}</span></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="k">Gas</span><span class="v">£0</span></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="k">Gas standing charge</span><span class="v">£0</span></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="k"><strong>Total</strong></span><span class="v"><strong>{gbp(a.with_hp_total)}</strong></span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
    That leaves <strong>{gbp(a.saving_at_market_gas)} a year</strong>, which is still a saving but
    a lot less than the brochure figure. If someone quotes you a heat pump saving close to your
    whole gas bill, they've probably left out the electricity.
  </p>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>Payback</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    This depends on what you compare it with, and published payback figures tend to use whichever
    comparison looks best. All three below use the market gas price.
  </p>

  <table class="spec">
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>{p.at_market.raw} years</td>
        <td><strong>If the old boiler would have run forever, free.</strong> {gbp(cost.paid_gbp)}
        divided by {gbp(a.saving_at_market_gas)}. The simplest and most cautious figure.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>{p.at_market.net_of_boiler} years</td>
        <td><strong>If you net off the boiler you'd have bought anyway.</strong> Ours is 19 years
        old and heat-only. A like-for-like replacement is around {gbp(cost.avoided_boiler_gbp)} and
        would have been needed within a few years anyway, so only {gbp(netCapital)} of this is
        extra spending.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>~{p.at_market.realistic} years</td>
        <td><strong>Our best estimate.</strong> The boiler didn't need replacing now, just at some
        point in the next few years. Money spent now to avoid a cost in year four doesn't cancel it
        pound for pound, so we've discounted it, which gives roughly this.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>We use the middle figure.</strong> We could have quoted
    {' '}{p.at_market.net_of_boiler} years, but the boiler we avoided buying was a future cost, and
    counting it as money already saved inflates the payback. Around {p.at_market.realistic} years
    is the figure we're comfortable with. On our fixed gas price it would be
    {' '}{p.at_fixed.realistic}, and since that fix ends in March we don't use it as the baseline.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>If gas prices change</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    Keeping the boiler means betting that gas stays cheap, and recently it hasn't. This table runs
    the same sum across a range of gas prices and real-world efficiencies. Our fix is the top row
    and today's market price is the second. The rows below show what happens if prices rise the way
    they have in the last few years.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Gas price</th>
          {s.scop.map((sc: number) => <th>SCOP {sc.toFixed(1)}</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {s.rows.map((r: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <strong>{r.gas_p.toFixed(2)}p</strong>
              {r.label ? <span class="muted"> · {r.label}</span> : null}
            </td>
            {r.saving.map((v: number) => (
              <td style={`background:${shade(v)}; color:${ink(v)}; font-family:var(--mono); font-size:0.82rem`}>
                {gbp(v)}
              </td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="lede">
    At 9p gas, which is quite possible over the twenty-year life of a heat pump, the saving is
    {' '}{gbp(s.rows[3].saving[1])} a year and the raw payback drops below five years. Each rise
    in wholesale gas prices makes the boiler look worse, and ours is near the end of its life
    anyway.
  </p>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>Turning the flow temperature down</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    Octopus's standard install is commissioned at <strong>{hardware.flow_temp_c}°C</strong>. That's
    quick to set up and means fewer radiator changes, but it costs efficiency for as long as it's
    left there. The Cosy 9 manages SCOP 3.6 at 50°C, falling towards 3.0 at 65°C. On the market row
    above, going from SCOP 3.0 to 3.6 is worth about
    {' '}{gbp(marketRow.saving[2] - marketRow.saving[0])} a year, and more if gas goes up. It only
    takes an afternoon of adjusting and accepting longer run times. We'll do it in spring rather
    than at commissioning in December, because a cold house in December is a bad way to find out
    we've turned it down too far.
  </p>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>Why it works here</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    That {gbp(a.saving_at_market_gas)} isn't what a heat pump would save a typical house. It's what
    it saves in <em>this</em> one, where the
    {' '}{demand.hp_electricity_kwh.toLocaleString('en-GB')} kWh of extra winter demand is covered by
    10.45 kWp of panels, a 15 kWh battery and an overnight rate under 8p. Put the same unit in a
    house on a flat tariff with no battery and it would barely beat gas.
  </p>

  <div class="note quiet">
    <p><strong>The same house with no solar and no battery</strong>, running this heat pump and the
    car on the same tariff, would pay about <strong>{gbp(a.no_solar_no_battery)}</strong> a year
    for electricity, because none of it could be moved into the cheap window. Without the panels,
    battery and tariff, the heat pump would only just pay its way. See
    {' '}<a href="/tariff/">the tariff</a> for why that window matters so much, and
    {' '}<a href="/ev/">the car</a> for the other half of the electrification bill.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>The kit</h2></div>
  <table class="spec">
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Unit</td><td>{hardware.model} monobloc, {hardware.refrigerant} (propane) refrigerant, with a global warming potential of 3 against 675 for the R32 alternative we were also quoted.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sized for</td><td>{hardware.heat_loss_kw} kW surveyed heat loss. The Cosy 9 covers up to 8.7 kW at 50°C flow.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cylinder</td><td>{hardware.cylinder_litres} L slimline unvented, replacing the old vented tank. The loft header tank comes out with it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Radiators</td><td>{hardware.radiators_upgraded} upgraded to type 22 K2, included in the fixed price. The rest of the house keeps what it has.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Underfloor</td><td>Already there, already decommissioned from gas, reconnected as part of the install. Underfloor heating works well at low flow temperatures, so it should suit the heat pump better than anything else in the house.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Price</td><td>{gbp(cost.list_gbp)} list, less the {gbp(cost.bus_grant_gbp)} Boiler Upgrade Scheme grant that Octopus claim on our behalf, leaving {gbp(cost.paid_gbp)}.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="card referral" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
    <p class="kicker">Thinking of switching?</p>
    <h2 style="margin:0.2rem 0 0"><img src="/octopus-mark.png" alt="" class="octopus-logo-inline" /> Octopus · we both get £50</h2>
    <p>The site has no sponsors. The referral links are the only money it makes, and you get the
    same amount we do. You don't need solar or a heat pump; it works for any tariff.</p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn octopus" href={OCTOPUS_REFERRAL} rel="noopener"><img src="/octopus-mark.png" alt="Octopus Energy" class="octopus-mark" /> Claim £50</a>
      <p class="fine mono">share.octopus.energy/lush-spark-49</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head"><h2>What we'll know in March</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    The most important figures on this page are estimates. Heat demand is worked out from gas
    readings instead of being measured at the radiators. SCOP is the manufacturer's figure, not a
    reading from our house in a cold spell. The gas price will keep changing, and our fix shields us
    from that until March 2027. A real January will give us the first two, and the market will
    decide the third. We'll publish the updated figures either way.
  </p>
</Base>
